Task 1: Prepare the System Environment
Subtask 1.1: Update System and Install Dependencies
First, let's ensure our system is up-to-date and has all required dependencies.

# Update the system packages
sudo dnf update -y

# Install required packages
sudo dnf install -y python3 python3-pip git wget curl

# Install additional development tools
sudo dnf groupinstall -y "Development Tools"

# Install PostgreSQL (required for Private Automation Hub)
sudo dnf install -y postgresql postgresql-server postgresql-contrib

# Install Redis (required for task queue management)
sudo dnf install -y redis

# Install Nginx (for reverse proxy)
sudo dnf install -y nginx
Subtask 1.2: Configure PostgreSQL Database
The Private Automation Hub requires a PostgreSQL database for storing metadata and user information.

# Initialize PostgreSQL database
sudo postgresql-setup --initdb

# Start and enable PostgreSQL service
sudo systemctl start postgresql
sudo systemctl enable postgresql

# Switch to postgres user and create database
sudo -u postgres psql << EOF
CREATE DATABASE automationhub;
CREATE USER automationhub WITH PASSWORD 'secure_password123';
GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON DATABASE automationhub TO automationhub;
ALTER USER automationhub CREATEDB;
\q
EOF
Subtask 1.3: Configure Redis Service
Redis is used for caching and task queue management.

# Start and enable Redis service
sudo systemctl start redis
sudo systemctl enable redis

# Verify Redis is running
redis-cli ping
Expected output: PONG

Task 2: Install Private Automation Hub
Subtask 2.1: Create Installation Directory and User
# Create a dedicated user for automation hub
sudo useradd -r -s /bin/bash -d /opt/automationhub automationhub

# Create installation directory
sudo mkdir -p /opt/automationhub
sudo chown automationhub:automationhub /opt/automationhub

# Switch to automation hub user
sudo su - automationhub
Subtask 2.2: Install Galaxy NG (Open Source Private Automation Hub)
Since we're using open-source tools, we'll install galaxy_ng, which is the open-source version of Private Automation Hub.

# Create Python virtual environment
python3 -m venv /opt/automationhub/venv
source /opt/automationhub/venv/bin/activate

# Upgrade pip
pip install --upgrade pip

# Install galaxy_ng and dependencies
pip install galaxy-ng[ldap]
pip install gunicorn
pip install psycopg2-binary

# Install additional required packages
pip install django-cors-headers
pip install pulp-ansible
Subtask 2.3: Configure Galaxy NG Settings
Create the configuration file for Galaxy NG:

# Create settings directory
mkdir -p /opt/automationhub/config

# Create the main settings file
cat > /opt/automationhub/config/settings.py << 'EOF'
import os
from galaxy_ng.app.settings.base import *

# Database configuration
DATABASES = {
    'default': {
        'ENGINE': 'django.db.backends.postgresql',
        'NAME': 'automationhub',
        'USER': 'automationhub',
        'PASSWORD': 'secure_password123',
        'HOST': 'localhost',
        'PORT': '5432',
    }
}

# Redis configuration
REDIS_URL = "redis://localhost:6379/0"
CACHES = {
    'default': {
        'BACKEND': 'django_redis.cache.RedisCache',
        'LOCATION': REDIS_URL,
        'OPTIONS': {
            'CLIENT_CLASS': 'django_redis.client.DefaultClient',
        }
    }
}

# Security settings
SECRET_KEY = 'your-secret-key-change-this-in-production'
DEBUG = False
ALLOWED_HOSTS = ['*']

# Static files
STATIC_URL = '/static/'
STATIC_ROOT = '/opt/automationhub/static/'

# Media files
MEDIA_URL = '/media/'
MEDIA_ROOT = '/opt/automationhub/media/'

# Galaxy NG specific settings
GALAXY_API_ROOT = '/api/galaxy/'
CONTENT_ORIGIN = 'http://localhost:8080'

# Authentication
GALAXY_AUTHENTICATION_CLASSES = [
    'galaxy_ng.app.auth.session.SessionAuthentication',
    'ansible_base.authentication.authenticators.BasicAuthentication',
]

# CORS settings
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS = [
    "http://localhost:8080",
    "http://127.0.0.1:8080",
]

# Logging
